<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1893 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Kaulana Nā Pua</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hawaiian song from 1893 for band players who refused to swear loyalty to the new government after their queen was overthrown.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ellen Kehoʻohiwaokalani Wright Prendergast</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ellen Kehoʻohiwaokalani Wright Prendergast</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Native Hawaiians; the Royal Hawaiian Band</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Hawaiʻi</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1893</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Ellen Kehoʻohiwaokalani Wright Prendergast (1893))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaulana-na-pua">Kaulana Nā Pua</a>&quot; (&quot;Famous Are the Flowers&quot;) is a Hawaiian patriotic song written in 1893 by Ellen Kehoʻohiwaokalani Wright Prendergast. It is also known as &quot;Mele ʻAi Pōhaku&quot; (&quot;Stone-Eating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;) and &quot;Mele Aloha ʻĀina&quot; (&quot;Patriot's Song&quot;).</p><p>In 1893 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> was overthrown and the Hawaiian Kingdom ended. Those who seized power proclaimed the Provisional Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, which later became the Republic of Hawaii and then a territory and a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Members of the Royal Hawaiian Band refused to accept it: when their bandmaster demanded that they sign an oath of loyalty to the Provisional Government, they walked out of their jobs. As the political scientist Noenoe Silva of the University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa has explained, the bandmaster told them that if they did not sign they would be eating rocks. Prendergast wrote the song for these players. Its words express deep opposition to annexation by the United States.</p><p>According to Samuel H. Elbert and Noelani Mahoe (1970), the song was considered sacred and not for dancing. Today, however, hālau hula perform it as a hula ʻauana, a graceful dance for mature women. It remains popular in Hawaiʻi, though it is not clear how many listeners who do not speak Hawaiian know its history. The Hawaiian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> has given it new significance, and its words are often cited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaiian-sovereignty-movement">Hawaiian sovereignty movement</a> as an expression of opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> rule.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of loyalty to the Hawaiian land and to its overthrown queen. It praises the band players who refused the new government's oath, rejects trading loyalty to one's country for money, and holds that the land will go on feeding those who stay faithful to it. The mood is proud and defiant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hawaiian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hawaiian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kaulana nā pua aʻo Hawaiʻi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Famous are the children of Hawaiʻi,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kūpaʻa mahope o ka ʻāina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>steadfast behind the land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Hiki mai ka ʻelele o ka loko ʻino</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Here comes the messenger of the evil heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Palapala ʻānunu me ka pākaha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a greedy document of extortion.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Pane mai Hawaiʻi moku o Keawe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hawaiʻi, island of Keawe, answers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kōkua nā Hono aʻo Piʻilani</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the bays of Piʻilani help,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kākoʻo mai Kauaʻi o Mano</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Kauaʻi of Mano supports,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Paʻapū me ke one Kākuhihewa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>united firmly with the sands of Kākuhihewa.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻAʻole aʻe kau i ka pūlima</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No one will put the signature</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Maluna o ka pepa o ka ʻenemi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on the paper of the enemy:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Hoʻohui ʻāina kūʻai hewa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>annexation, the wrongful sale</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">I ka pono sivila aʻo ke kanaka</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of the civil rights of the people.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻAʻole mākou aʻe minamina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We do not value</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">I ka puʻukālā a ke aupuni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the government's heap of money;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Ua lawa mākou i ka pōhaku</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we are content with the stones,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">I ka ʻai kamahaʻo o ka ʻāina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the wondrous food of the land.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Mahope mākou o Liliʻulani</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We stand behind Liliʻulani</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">A loaʻa e ka pono o ka ʻāina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>until the rights of the land are won.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Haʻina ʻia mai ana ka puana</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Told is the refrain:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Ka poʻe i aloha i ka ʻāina</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the people who love the land.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ellen Kehoʻohiwaokalani Wright Prendergast (1893); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (Hawaiian Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://haw.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaulana_N%C4%81_Pua" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'pua', literally 'flowers', is the usual figure for children, descendants.</li><li>line 1.4: 'pākaha', to plunder, fleece, extort.</li><li>line 2.1: the island of Hawaiʻi, called after the chief Keawe.</li><li>line 2.2: 'nā Hono a Piʻilani', the bays of Piʻilani, i.e. Maui (with Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi and Kahoʻolawe), after the Maui chief Piʻilani.</li><li>line 2.3: Kauaʻi of Mano (Manokalanipō), its ancient chief.</li><li>line 2.4: the sands of Kākuhihewa, i.e. Oʻahu, after the Oʻahu chief Kākuhihewa.</li><li>line 3.3: 'Hoʻohui ʻāina', the joining of lands, annexation; 'kūʻai hewa', a wrongful sale or bargain.</li><li>line 4.3: 'pōhaku', stones; the stones are the people's own food, the 'wondrous food of the land', rather than the government's money.</li><li>line 5.1: 'Liliʻulani' as written (Queen Liliʻuokalani).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
